jobs.body:
Under the overall supervision of the Regional Director and the direct supervision of the IOM Ghana (resident), Togo and Benin Chief of Mission and with direct technical support from the Regional Thematic Specialist for the Immigration and Border Management (IBM) as well as the IBM Team in HQ, the Programme Officer (Immigration and Border Governance (IBG)) will be accountable and responsible for the overall development, management, coordination and oversight of the IBM projects portfolio and strategy in the Gulf of Guinea Region to strengthen the capacity of border management agencies and reinforce the resilience of border communities to respond to emerging challenges in border management, contributing to stronger migration governance and border security in the region.

**Responsibilities and Accountabilities**

1. Manage the IBM regional projects for the Gulf of Guinea (specifically in Benin, Côte d’Ivoire, Ghana, Togo, and Mauritania) in an effective and timely manner, in line with IOM's policies and practices as well as donor requirements, including ensuring the integration of new modalities, practices, and methods for evaluation into the project formulation process, monitoring and assessing project implementation activities, and taking corrective or alternative action for improvement and success.
2. Provide technical inputs for the development of policy and operational resources aimed at enhancing safe, orderly, and regular migration pathways through the implementation of immigration and border governance policies and tools.
3. Provide guidance and substantive support to the Regional Office and Country Offices (COs), in coordination with the IBG Regional Thematic Specialist (RTS), on emerging national and regional trends, donor priorities, and resource mobilization for the development and execution of IBG projects.
4. Provide operational guidance and technical supervision on IBG projects, including managing and providing technical support to relevant programme staff, and capacity development initiatives.
5. Coordinate and conduct capacity-development activities in coordination with technical experts on IBG thematic areas including, but not limited to, document security, passport examination, legal identity, identity management, the responsible use of biometrics, readmission, cooperation, admission and stay pathways, and Advance Passenger Information, amongst others.
6. Establish and maintain close and effective working relationships with government counterparts at the technical level, to guide project implementation and the resolution of implementation-related challenges, including organizing periodical meetings with relevant stakeholders.
7. Oversee the elaboration of public information and training materials, including resources for customs, border and immigration authorities, visa officers, and other border governance stakeholders for internal and external use, in coordination with IBG Specialists and other relevant Divisions.
8. Pursue the establishment of partnerships with UN Agencies, humanitarian organizations, government, and community interlocutors, and with a broad range of stakeholders towards the successful implementation of IBG programmes.
9. Contribute to advance IBG thematic areas within the respective UN Network on Migration (UNNM) and other initiatives related to institutional efforts to implement the Global Compact for Migration (GCM) and strengthen migration governance.
10. Undertake travel duty as required to strengthen, coordinate and/or support programme implementation.
11. Perform any such duties as may be assigned.

**Required Qualifications and Experience**

**Education**

- Master’s degree in International Relations, Political Science, Humanitarian Affairs, Economic Development, Business or Public Administration, International Studies or a related field from an accredited academic institution with five years of relevant professional experience; or,
- University degree in the above fields with seven years of relevant professional experience.

Accredited Universities are those listed in the UNESCO World Higher Education Database.

**Experience**

- Experience in programme conceptualisation, development, monitoring, reporting and evaluation;
- Experience in programme development and capacity-development activities;
- Experience in liaising with governmental and diplomatic authorities as well as with national and international institutions;
- Familiarity with financial and business administration;
- Sound and proven understanding of internal and international migration issues in the country and in the Region;
- Experience in organizing consultations, workshops and seminars with high-level stakeholders;
- In-depth knowledge of the broad range of migration-related subject areas dealt with by the Organization; and,
- Knowledge of UN and bilateral donor programming.

**Skills**

- Proven ability to establish and maintain strong working relations with relevant Government counterparts, international organizations and private sector entities;
- Excellent leadership, coordination, and information management skills;
- Detailed knowledge of the UN System, NGOs and other international organizations/institutions;
- Knowledge of migration data, labour migration patterns in the relevant region and ability to negotiate with government authorities;
- Good knowledge of human and financial resources management;
- Strong conceptualization, analytical and writing skills;
- Strong communication, organizational and interpersonal relationship skills;
- Ability to meet deadlines and work under pressure with minimum supervision; and,
- Ability to establish and maintain partnerships with development partners, government counterparts, UN agencies, local authorities and other relevant stakeholders.

**Languages**

For this position, fluency in English and French is required (oral and written).

Working knowledge of another official UN languages (Arabic, Chinese, Russian, and Spanish) is an advantage.

Proficiency of language(s) required will be specifically evaluated during the selection process, which may include written and/or oral assessments.

## GENERAL INFORMATION

CRF PROJECT CODE AND PROJECT TITLE PF272 – Ready 360°  
ACTIVITY TITLE R3.A3 Dissemination of messages through targeted media (TV, radio, social media, outdoor advertising, etc.) as part of the development of the campaign's awareness-raising activities, including awareness messages and the Caribbean song on disaster risk prevention and climate change.

ACTIVITY LOCATION 8 Caribbean territories: Antigua and Barbuda, Dominica, Grenada, Guadeloupe, Martinique, Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Lucia, and Saint Vincent and the Grenadines

ACTIVITY MANAGERS (NAME AND POSITION) Emmeline Da Silva (Communications Manager) and Sandrine Désiré (Regional Project Manager)  
PLANNED PERIOD / DATES From 15 november 2026 to 15 march 2027

### 1. CONTEXT AND RATIONALE

1.1 PIRAC

Established in 2005, PIRAC (Regional Intervention Platform for the Americas and the Caribbean) is the representative delegation of the French Red Cross (FRC) for the Caribbean region, based in Guadeloupe. It is also the operational mechanism through which the French Red Cross implements humanitarian action and regional cooperation.

PIRAC supports Caribbean populations affected by major disasters and works to strengthen the resilience and preparedness capacities of populations in the French territories and countries of the Caribbean arc.

1.2 The READY 360° / 3 Oceans Programme

The **READY 360° / 3 Oceans programme** aims to build comprehensive and greener resilience across Caribbean territories by strengthening disaster preparedness and response capacities at all levels, from individuals and families, to organisations and businesses, and up to regional institutional coordination for disaster risk management.  
The programme's institutional partners are the **Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States (OECS) and the Caribbean Disaster Emergency Management Agency (CDEMA).**

Eight Caribbean territories are involved in the programme:

- Antigua and Barbuda;
- Dominica;
- Grenada;
- Guadeloupe;
- Martinique;
- Saint Kitts and Nevis;
- Saint Lucia;
- Saint Vincent and the Grenadines.

One of the expected outcomes of the programme is to contribute to strengthening the resilience of Caribbean citizens through improved information, awareness and preparedness for disasters and crises.

As part of the programme, several activities have been implemented to strengthen risk awareness and the resilience of Caribbean populations to natural hazards and the impacts of climate change, by ensuring the broad dissemination of disaster risk awareness messages and increasing the visibility of tools developed under the READY 360° / 3 Oceans programme.

These activities notably include the regional awareness campaign “**It's Your Move!**” and the regional **Caribbean Risks** portal.

The *It's Your Move!* campaign uses a range of awareness-raising materials, including infographics, testimonial videos and posters, to make preparedness messages accessible and relevant to the general public in Caribbean territories. These materials are available in French and English.

The Caribbean Risks portal was developed as a regional reference resource for the general public. It brings together reliable, accessible information adapted to the realities of Caribbean territories, with the aim of informing, raising awareness and helping citizens prepare for the main natural hazards to which they are exposed.

TV and radio awareness spots are also currently being produced and are scheduled for broadcast in November/ December.

1.3 The Awareness-Raising Song

As part of the awareness campaign, an original song and music video will be developed as awareness-raising tools targeting audiences across the region. The objective is to raise awareness among local populations, particularly young people and families, about major natural hazards and the impacts of climate change in the Caribbean, and subsequently direct audiences towards reliable resources such as the campaign and the portal.

An original choreography is integrated into the music video and is intended to become a recognisable and easily reproducible element of the campaign.

The official release date of the song is currently scheduled for 11 January 2027.

The song, primarily in English, is intended for regional distribution. It will notably be made available on major music streaming and distribution platforms.

As part of an overall promotion strategy for the song, PIRAC wishes to develop an activation involving micro-influencers in order to launch a TikTok / Instagram Reels challenge around the choreography.

The objective is to enable creators to initiate the challenge within their communities and subsequently encourage users to reproduce the choreography themselves and publish their own videos.  
This activation is one component of the overall song promotion strategy, which also includes media relations, media programming and broadcasting activities.

### 2. OBJECTIVES OF THE ASSIGNMENT

2.1 OVERALL OBJECTIVE

The objective of the assignment is to launch and amplify a regional challenge around the song's choreography, by mobilising micro-influencers with primarily local audiences in the territories concerned.

The activation should aim to:

- raise awareness of the song among Caribbean audiences;
- establish the choreography as recognisable and easy-to-reproduce content;
- encourage users to create and publish their own videos;
- generate user-generated content around the choreography;
- maximise the reach and visibility of the song at regional level.

1. GEOGRAPHICAL SCOPE

The activation will cover the following eight territories :

- Guadeloupe;
- Martinique;
- Antigua and Barbuda;
- Dominica;
- Grenada;
- Saint Kitts and Nevis;
- Saint Lucia;
- Saint Vincent and the Grenadines.

The service provider must be able to propose a strategy ensuring coverage across all territories.

PIRAC does not set a minimum number of creators per territory. The service provider shall propose the  
combination of profiles it considers most relevant to ensure regional coverage and broad reach, taking into account in particular:

- the size and engagement of local communities;
- platform usage patterns;
- the availability of relevant micro-influencers;
- the specific characteristics of each territory;
- the resources available for the activation.

The service provider may mobilise its own creator network and/or work with local partners.

The proposal must specify which territories are covered by the service provider's own network and, where applicable, which partners it intends to involve.

The campaign targets both French-speaking and English-speaking territories. The service provider must therefore demonstrate its ability to mobilise creators in both linguistic environments.

1. TARGET AUDIENCES AND CREATOR PROFILES

The campaign primarily targets local audiences in the territories concerned, with particular attention to TikTok users, with a **pre-targeted age range of 15–25**. Instagram may also be activated as a secondary platform, depending on the creators selected and the available budget.

The assignment will primarily rely on the mobilisation of local micro-influencers/content creators.  
Follower count will not be the sole selection criterion. Particular attention will be paid to:

- the creator's local presence and connection to the territory;
- the composition and geographical location of their audience;
- their community engagement rate;
- their ability to generate participation;
- their relevance to the target audiences;
- their editorial style and ability to create natural and authentic content.

From a strategic perspective, PIRAC favours mobilising **several micro-influencers with strong local roots**, rather than relying on a larger influencer with a broader audience, who would generally be more costly. This approach aims to ensure broader territorial coverage and greater proximity to local communities.

**This is a particularly important requirement**: the selected profiles must also be compatible with the **values, principles and image of the French Red Cross** (see the French Red Cross editorial guidelines attached as an annex).

Micro-influencers must not currently create, have previously created, or collaborate with companies promoting violence, weapons or drugs, or disseminating degrading images or racist, homophobic, ableist or sexist content.

The service provider shall submit proposed profiles to PIRAC for prior approval before any contractual commitment is made or content is published.

Applicants may include in their proposal an initial indicative selection of profiles that could potentially be mobilised, specifying in particular their territory, main platform, audience size and location and, where available, their key engagement indicators.

### 5. PLATFORMS

**TikTok will be the priority platform** for the activation.  
Instagram may also be activated, particularly through the Reels format.  
Where relevant and technically possible, the same content may be published on both TikTok and Instagram in order to maximise reach.

### 6. ACTIVATION MECHANICS

As the choreography has already been created and integrated into the official music video, **no choreography creation is expected as part of this assignment.**

The service provider will primarily be responsible for organising and coordinating the mobilisation of creators around the existing choreography.

The intended mechanism is based on the following principle:

***Mobilised creators → choreography published → communities encouraged to reproduce the choreography → users create and publish their own videos → song and challenge amplified***.

The service provider may propose additional measures to strengthen this dynamic, provided that they remain simple, adapted to platform usage and proportionate to the resources available.

### 7. SERVICES EXPECTED

#### 7.1 ACTIVATION STRATEGY

The service provider shall:

- propose a regional activation strategy;
- define a launch and distribution mechanism for the challenge;
- recommend the most relevant number and combination of creators;
- propose a distribution of profiles across the different territories;
- identify priority profiles to be mobilised;
- propose an operational activation timeline;
- define mechanisms to encourage users to reproduce the choreography.

#### 7.2 CONTENT PRODUCTION AND DISTRIBUTION

For each creator mobilised, the activation shall include, as a minimum:

- 1 TikTok video reproducing the choreography;
- use of the song's audio track;
- use of the hashtag defined for the challenge;
- tagging of the campaign accounts according to the arrangements agreed with PIRAC;
- a call to action encouraging users to reproduce the choreography and publish their own video.

Where platform features and usage allow, content may be cross-posted on TikTok and Instagram in order to maximise reach.

### 7.3 ACTIVATION MANAGEMENT AND AMPLIFICATION

The service provider shall monitor the activation and, where necessary, propose actions to maintain the momentum of the challenge throughout the agreed campaign period.

This may include identifying relevant user-generated content and recommending opportunities for organic amplification.

Any additional paid amplification or sponsored activity shall be presented separately and require prior approval from PIRAC. The service provider may include recommendations regarding this aspect in its proposal.

### 8. MATERIALS PROVIDED BY PIRAC

PIRAC will provide the service provider and selected creators with the materials required for the activation, including:

- the song;
- the official music video;
- the choreography;
- campaign visual identity elements;
- logos and required graphic assets;
- campaign accounts and hashtags to be tagged or used;
- background information and awareness-raising content required to understand the project.

The precise terms and conditions governing the use of content and graphic assets will be agreed with the selected service provider.

### 9. TIMELINE

Production of the song and music video will be completed by the end of November. The song, music video and choreography may be provided to the service provider in advance for its outreach and discussions with influencers.

The public release is scheduled for **11 January 2027.**

The promotion campaign will run for one month.

The service provider shall propose a detailed timeline in its offer, specifying in particular:

- the preparation phase;
- identification and approval of creators;
- contracting;
- launch of the activation;
- main publication period;
- follow-up and amplification activities;
- performance monitoring;
- deadline for submission of the final report.

The service provider may recommend a different activation period if it considers that this would maximise reach and participation.

### 10. OBJECTIVES AND PERFORMANCE INDICATORS

PIRAC does not set a minimum number of publications or guaranteed views. The performance of a micro-influencer activation depends in particular on the communities mobilised, platform algorithms and thespontaneous participation of users.

The service provider must nevertheless propose realistic means-based objectives and performance indicators in its offer to assess the effectiveness of the activation.

**Deployment indicators**

Monitoring should notably measure:

- number of creators mobilised;
- number of territories covered;
- number of pieces of content published;
- number of platforms activated;
- number of publications on TikTok and Instagram;
- number of cross-posted publications, where relevant.

**Performance indicators**

Where data is available, the service provider shall monitor:

- estimated reach;
- number of views;
- impressions;
- engagement rate;
- number of interactions;
- number of videos created organically by people who were not part of the mobilised influencer network;
- key insights allowing PIRAC to assess the momentum generated around the challenge.

The ability of the activation to generate significant regional reach will be a key element in assessing campaign performance.

### 11. EXPECTED DELIVERABLES

The service provider shall provide, at a minimum:

**Before the activation**

- a regional activation strategy;
- a proposed combination of creators and territorial allocation;
- a qualified list of profiles submitted to PIRAC for approval;
- an operational timeline;
- activation monitoring arrangements.

**During the activation**

- a tracking table of creators and publications;
- monitoring of content actually published;
- monitoring of content performance;
- identification of relevant user-generated content;
- progress updates with PIRAC, according to the agreed arrangements.

**After the activation**

A final report including, in particular:

- creators actually mobilised;
- territories covered;
- content published;
- platforms used;
- performance achieved;
- reach and number of views;
- available interactions and engagement rates;
- user-generated content identified;
- key lessons learned and recommendations for future activations.

### 12. BUDGET AND FINANCIAL CONDITIONS

PIRAC wishes to prioritise an activation proportionate to the nature and scale of the campaign, while maximising regional reach and keeping costs under control.

The budget allocated to collaborations with micro-influencers is estimated at approximately **€4,000.**

This envelope corresponds to the amounts allocated to creator collaborations and is separate from the service provider's fees.

Applicants shall submit a detailed financial offer clearly distinguishing between:

- their fees for strategy, selection, coordination, monitoring and reporting;
- expected remuneration or costs related to creators;
- any other costs required to implement the assignment.

The applicant shall propose an organisation that maximises the regional reach and visibility of the campaign within the available resources.

Any additional costs must be clearly identified in the offer and subject to prior approval by PIRAC.

### 13. REQUIRED CAPABILITIES AND EXPERIENCE

Applicants must demonstrate:

- experience in implementing micro-influencer campaigns;
- strong knowledge of TikTok and Instagram Reels;
- ability to mobilise creators with local audiences;
- knowledge of social media usage patterns in the Caribbean;
- ability to work across multiple territories;
- ability to mobilise French-speaking and English-speaking creators, either directly or through local partners;
- experience coordinating multiple creators;
- ability to monitor and analyse campaign performance.

Applicants shall specify in their offer:

- the territories in which they have a direct creator network;
- any local partners they intend to involve;
- their experience in the territories concerned;
- the number and type of profiles they would be able to mobilise;
- examples of similar campaigns carried out.

### 14. ORGANISATION AND COORDINATION WITH PIRAC

The service provider will work in close coordination with PIRAC, particularly with the Communications Manager.

PIRAC will notably be responsible for:

- approving the proposed strategy;
- approving creator profiles before they are mobilised;
- providing the materials required for the activation;
- approving key communication elements where necessary.

The service provider will be responsible for the operational coordination with the creators mobilised and for ensuring compliance with the agreed timeline.

### 15. SELECTION CRITERIA

Criteria Indicative weighting

Understanding of the objectives and relevance of the proposed methodology 30%

References and experience in the Caribbean region 10%

Quality and breadth of the professional network 20%

Price / value for money 30%

Timeline and proposed schedule 10%

TOTAL 100%

Head of Consortium DOVIRA (Developing Organized Vital Interventions for Relief and Assistance of the most vulnerable populations in Ukraine)

**Country Programme:** Ukraine

**Base:** Kyiv, Ukraine with frequent field trips

**Line Manager:** Country Director

**People in Need** (PIN) is a Czech-based international non-governmental organization (NGO) that has provided humanitarian and development assistance across more than 20 countries since 1992. Today, PIN stands as one of the largest organizations of its kind in Central and Eastern Europe, supporting vulnerable communities in Asia, Africa, the Middle East, and Eastern Europe. PIN empowers civil society, advocates for human rights, and promotes social inclusion both at home and abroad.

## **About the role:**

Head of Consortium (HoC) provides leadership and strategic guidance for the management and direction of the Consortium DOVIRA programme to achieve expected programmatic results within budget and timeframe; oversees operational delivery (including assurance of compliance with the donor as well as PIN requirements and regulations); facilitates the strategic development of the consortium programme; and strengthens relationships among consortium partners (Caritas, Relief Coordination Centre, New Way, Angels of Salvation, EcoClub), donors, and other stakeholders.

## **About DOVIRA:**

DOVIRA programme aims to enhance the resilience and well-being of war-affected communities across East, West, North and South Ukraine by improving access to adequate housing, mental health and psychosocial support, safe drinking water, hygiene assistance and livelihood opportunities.

**Internal relations:**

The HoC reports to the PIN Country Director and directly manages Deputy Head of Consortium**,** coordinating closely with other PIN units’ focal points to ensure effective and efficient award implementation (programme quality, MEAL, grants and partnership management, compliance, finance).

**External relations:**

The HoC works with a high degree of autonomy and is responsible for regularly coordinating with the donor as well as for facilitating the Consortium management related processes (coordination of the Consortium Steering Committee as well as Project Management Unit).

The HoC represents the Consortium as needed at external fora and General Coordination Meetings in close coordination with PIN Programme Quality and Development team, as well as Area teams. The HoC is also the principal point of contact for the main DOVIRA donor, US Department of State in-country, for programmatic related matters and for representative duties, as needed, including for advocacy and communications initiatives as delegated by CD.

## **Main Duties & Responsibilities:**

**Project Cycle Management**

- Responsible for the overall quality work of the Consortium Partners, through the management of the Deputy Head of Consortium, directly responsible for oversight and monitoring of the partners programme implementation;
- Accountable for planning, action design and delivery of objectives, strategies, plans and budgets of the Consortium led Programme;
- Ensures the delivery of agreed outputs of the Consortium and that these outputs comply with both internal and external quality standards;
- Ensures proper management and compliance oversight of all joint aspects of the Consortium, including financial and human resources;
- Liaises between different components of the Consortium whilst being neutral to all Consortium partners.

**Compliance, Budget and Finance**

- Advises PIN and Consortium Partners, where needed, on donor’s compliance, rules and requirements in coordination with PIN HQ and other relevant units;
- Ensures that financial and contractual obligations are followed according to US Department of States rules and regulations;
- Ensures that operational and administrative procedures are followed according to US Department of State guidelines, MoUs and cooperation agreements between PIN and Consortium Partners;
- In close collaboration with PIN Finance focal point, coordinates with partner Finance and Operations focal points to problem solve as needed, approve changes and revisions to budgets, and to ensure that partner contracting and supervision processes across the Consortium are effective and in line with PIN and donors requirements;
- Ensures effective risk management and conflict-sensitive programming; Escalates significant risks and strategic decisions to CD and the Steering Committee when required.

**Communication and Coordination**

- Main focal point for all Consortium related partnerships and stakeholders, including managing the relationship with the US Department of State in-country as related to the programme;
- Responsible for development of specific Consortium governance documents (management framework, establishment of coordination mechanisms and developing relevant Terms of References for governing bodies – Steering Committee, Project Management Unit, sectorial-focused meetings and reviews etc);
- Facilitates the Consortium’s Steering Committee’s work and organizes regular meetings with the aim to enable efficient coordination of program activities and effective learning;
- Responsible for establishing close collaboration among Lead Agency departments involved in the programme implementation and oversight, ensuring clear definition of their roles and responsibilities related to DOVIRA programme;
- Strategizes on the engagement of national and international stakeholders in the action, including representing the Consortium in coordination mechanisms and various advocacy fora;
- Ensures a high quality of communication among the Consortium partners and, in close coordination with PIN’s Advocacy and Communication Director, provides professional recommendations and relevant data to strengthen advocacy efforts of the Consortium action;
- Oversees the development of case studies and relevant communication materials and share and promote these;
- Acts as the spokesperson for the Consortium with high-level stakeholders nationally and internationally as appropriate.

**Programme Development and Quality**

- Accountable for quality assurance of performance measures (e.g. outcome indicators, interim milestones, and the efficacy of the overall monitoring and evaluation systems);
- Exercises a strategic leadership on capacity development, identifying requirements for organizational capacity building and the development and implementation of capacity building activities;
- Responsible for resource mobilisation and positioning to other donors, that would ensure continuation of DOVIRA programme implementation.

**Grants Management, overseen in coordination with PIN Grants Management focal point**

- Ensures that contractual obligations are met in terms of programme deliverables;
- Ensures that donor rules and procedures are understood and respected by all parties, sharing information and conducting training if required on proposed systems and processes for consortium management.

## **Requirements:**

- Prior experience in managing consortia or a comparable senior leadership position within a similar complex context or multisectoral programme
- Excellent networking, partnership-building and representation skills
- Demonstrated ability to facilitate collaboration, manage diverse stakeholder relationships, resolve operational challenges and support joint decision-making
- Excellent spoken and written English communication skills
- Knowledge of the institutional donor environment and experience with donor relations, strategic programme planning and external communications
- Experience working with national and international NGOs, donors, government authorities and humanitarian coordination mechanisms
- Experience in early recovery, development and humanitarian programming, preferably in the region
- University degree in a relevant field
- Familiarity with conflict settings
- Knowledge of Ukrainian is preferred
- Knowledge of Russian is an advantage
- Dynamic and results-oriented, with strong organizational, planning, critical analysis and problem-solving skills
- Reliable and self-reliant, with a responsible and systematic approach to work
- Ability to work in difficult conditions and under pressure
- Cultural sensitivity
- Strong ability and willingness to transfer knowledge
- Willingness to travel within the country

The recruitment is contingent upon confirmation of the funding.

## **We offer:**

- Working environment in a well-established humanitarian organization in a challenging context;
- **Starting Salary** based on PIN’s salary scale and HR policies, between 3300-3600 EUR;
- **Additional allowances include**:
    - Monthly Subsistence Allowance of 1440 EUR;
    - Monthly Hardship Allowance of 400 EUR;
    - Accommodation provided by PIN/Monthly Housing Allowance up to 1000 EUR;
- **Time off and Rest:**
    - 25 days of holiday annually;
    - 10 sick days
    - Rest & Recreation (R&R) policy in place: 3x 5 days of leave per year with 350 Eur lump sum allowance per each leave.
- **Travel benefits:**
    - Reimbursement of travel expenses with travel to the country of residence once every 6 months of work;
    - Reimbursement of costs of visas and vaccinations; assistance with the visa application procedure;
    - Reimbursement for the medical check-up before and after the deployment;
    - Travel health insurance covering medical care, incl. the repatriation;
- **Health and Well-being:**  
    24/7 Medical Support available online;  
    Free psychological consultations available online;
- **Professional Development:** Free access to e-learning platforms and PIN Learning Hub; PIN’s global Induction Training, and tailored capacity-building opportunities, including Hostile Environment Awareness Training (HEAT);

## **Security situation in Ukraine**

Humanitarian workers continue to operate throughout Ukraine, although the front-line areas are being shelled almost constantly, and main cities are being targeted with missiles and UAVs quite regularly. PIN security SOPs require PIN staff to proceed to shelter should the risk level be assessed by the Security Department as the highest (launch of missiles or drones) and remain there until the air raid siren is cancelled, which cumulatively can be several times a week, occasionally for several hours at a time (although shorter periods are as well occurring). While air strikes that were targeting energy, infrastructure have finished after winter, they have succeeded in reaching their goals and are likely to resume in late autumn/winter. At the moment military and civilian objects are being targeted, including the west of Ukraine. While Kyiv enjoys relatively strong air defence systems that are intercepting most of the missiles and UAVs, other regions are covered much less significantly which results in bigger damage. Outside of the areas in the vicinity to the contact line, services such as medical care, shops, public transportation as well as restaurants or leisure facilities remain operational.

*People in Need is committed to preventing any type of unwanted behaviour at work including sexual harassment, exploitation and abuse, lack of integrity and financial misconduct and is committed to promoting the welfare of children and adults with whom People in Need involved. People in Need expects all staff to perform job duties and responsibilities in accordance with People in Need code of conduct and key policies (available at:* [*https://www.peopleinneed.net/key-policies-4142gp*](https://www.peopleinneed.net/key-policies-4142gp)*). People in Need Staff will undertake the appropriate level of training.*

*All offers of employment will be subject to satisfactory references and appropriate screening checks, which can include criminal records and terrorism finance checks.*

*We are committed to ensuring diversity and gender equality within our organization and encourage applicants from diverse backgrounds to apply.*

## **Project Background**

**Green Value for Growth (GV4G)** is a five-year (60-month) Action funded by **the European Union and the Kingdom of the Netherlands** under the Global Gateway initiative. It aims to accelerate Mozambique’s transition towards greener, climate-resilient and inclusive development by transforming the **soy, cashew and coffee value chains** along the Beira and Nacala corridors. By 2031, GV4G aims to mobilise €27.7 million in public and private investment, support more than 2,500 businesses, cooperatives and commercial farms, reach 38,000 smallholder farmers, sustain 8,000 jobs and promote sustainable land management practices on nearly 36,000 hectares.

The programme is built around three outcomes: (1) modernising value chains and restoring sensitive landscapes; (2) strengthening SMEs and entrepreneurs in nature-positive value chains, particularly women and young people; (3) improving the regulatory, financial and digital environment for green investment (traceability, EUDR compliance, access to finance). It is implemented by a consortium of four partners: TechnoServe (lead partner, hosting the Programme Management Unit), Resilience, Nitidæ and the Zambezi Valley Development Agency (Agência de Desenvolvimento do Vale do Zambeze).

Within the consortium, Nitidæ is the **technical lead for the cashew value chain** and is responsible in particular for: supporting production through action research to drive the agroecological transition of cashew; strengthening the structuring of producer organisations; landscape governance on Mount Namuli to integrate the development of sustainable agroforestry coffee production; spatial analysis and deforestation monitoring (supported by Nitidæ’s N’Lab); and support for policy dialogue on the cashew sector.

## **Role of the Project Manager**

The Project Manager is responsible for the operational implementation of the activities entrusted to Nitidæ under the GV4G programme and for achieving their results. He/She coordinates the activities, the Nitidæ teams spread across Nampula, Zambézia and Manica provinces, and relations with consortium partners; oversees planning, budget monitoring and the production of deliverables; and ensures the quality of Nitidæ’s contributions to the technical and financial reporting consolidated by the Programme Management Unit.

He/She ensures that environmental and social requirements (climate resilience, landscape conservation, gender and youth inclusion) are mainstreamed across activities, and that diagnostics, consultations and implementation remain consistent. He/She is Nitidæ’s point of contact with consortium partners, authorities and private-sector value chain actors, and contributes to capitalising on lessons learned.

The position reports to Nitidæ’s Country Representative for Mozambique. The Project Manager works closely with field teams, the support services based in Maputo, thematic experts at headquarters (N’Lab, value chains, agroecology) and the managers of other cashew- and coffee-related projects. He/She will supervise a large team of more than 20 people, including field staff, with close support from several key positions within the team (policy/research coordinator, field coordinators, agroecology expert, M&E and GIS officer).

## **Responsibilities**

### **Planning, implementation, monitoring and evaluation**

- Plan the activities under Nitidæ’s responsibility with the team and in consultation with partners, in line with the consortium’s annual work plans; lead the start-up phase (recruitment, baseline studies, team set-up);
